Thank you.

18
00:01:08,310 --> 00:01:17,910
So, our topic and our focus today is testing ML models for enterprise use cases, enterprise products.

19
00:01:17,910 --> 00:01:26,631
Just to set context, could you explain the relationship between a data science model, an ML model, and an LLM?

20
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:32,469
Well, I would say that everybody's got their own vernacular about how all these things fit together,

21
00:01:32,469 --> 00:01:35,590
largely the way that I consider them.

22
00:01:35,590 --> 00:01:38,390
An LLM is just one type of ML model.

23
00:01:38,390 --> 00:01:42,630
And similarly, we use data science approaches to train various types of models,

24
00:01:42,630 --> 00:01:47,671
one of which could be an LLM, but they all have their sort of specific use cases. and applications.

25
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:51,670
Yeah, maybe just to build on that, Connor,

26
00:01:51,670 --> 00:01:55,270
when we think about the field of data science, I guess I could say traditionally, even.

27
00:01:55,270 --> 00:02:00,310
though it's a relatively new discipline, I think we see a lot of initial applications.

28
00:02:00,310 --> 00:02:04,630
that maybe grew almost out of the big data movement.

29
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:07,670
That was the key buzzword but 10, 15, 20 years ago, right?

30
00:02:07,670 --> 00:02:13,030
And we see things like teams of analysts inside a larger enterprise.

31
00:02:13,030 --> 00:02:19,589
that are developing models maybe to forecast revenue growth across market segments.

32
00:02:19,589 --> 00:02:25,110
And we have generally well ststructured inputs applied to a narrow range of questions.

33
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:27,190
and mostly for an internal audience.

34
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:29,750
And of course, there's a lot of people doing great work there.

35
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:33,910
And I don't mean to oversimplify how complex that kind of work can be.

36
00:02:33,910 --> 00:02:35,750
It's it's extremely hard stuff.

37
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:39,670
And forecasting revenues is pretty darn important for any company to get right.

38
00:02:39,670 --> 00:02:42,949
And I think what's really interesting now and what I think provokes this kind of conversation is.

39
00:02:42,959 --> 00:02:48,390
now we see the intense productization of those techniques at a greater scale,

40
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:52,949
especially in so far as they more and more approximate human intelligence.

41
00:02:52,949 --> 00:02:56,390
and therefore are justifiably called AI.

42
00:02:56,390 --> 00:03:00,070
So when we think about machine learning models in this context,

43
00:03:00,070 --> 00:03:06,309
we're thinking about things like accepting unstructured data. And the model is no longer a limited set of results.

44
00:03:06,309 --> 00:03:11,030
that are going to be curated and delivered in human time to a known audience.

45
00:03:11,030 --> 00:03:17,910
But it's going to be delivered in real time to wide audiences with consumer focuses without any human.

46
00:03:17,910 --> 00:03:20,550
in the loop checking on those results in the meantime.

47
00:03:20,550 --> 00:03:23,910
which of course exposes a whole host of concerns on the quality front.

48
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:25,750
Thanks for that, Jacob.

49
00:03:25,750 --> 00:03:28,309
So I think that leads me to my next question.

50
00:03:28,309 --> 00:03:37,190
Given this expanded focus, is that what leads companies to think of themselves as an AI first company.

51
00:03:37,190 --> 00:03:39,030
or a foundational AI company?

52
00:03:39,030 --> 00:03:41,030
And what is the relation between these two terms?

53
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:46,149
I think justifiably AI first companies are those.

54
00:03:46,149 --> 00:03:52,949
whose product really revolves around delivering value to their end customer through some kind of AI tooling.

55
00:03:52,949 --> 00:03:58,789
I think that the really useful designation or distinction that you brought up there is foundational versus not.

56
00:03:58,789 --> 00:04:00,949
So there are a lot of AI first companies.

57
00:04:00,949 --> 00:04:05,750
that are delivering really cool products that are built on top of other more foundational technologies.

58
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:08,789
And the difference between some of those companies.

59
00:04:08,789 --> 00:04:12,789
that are doing really neat things and a company like Deepgram Graham.

60
00:04:12,789 --> 00:04:15,990
or other big players in the space like OpenAI.

61
00:04:15,990 --> 00:04:18,949
and Enthropic is we're developing new models from scratch.

62
00:04:18,959 --> 00:04:23,350
maybe influenced by what's going on across the industry,

63
00:04:23,350 --> 00:04:27,189
informed by the latest developments in the research world, the academic world.

64
00:04:27,189 --> 00:04:30,790
but we're essentially developing new things from scratch,

65
00:04:30,790 --> 00:04:36,150
empowering other people to build all sorts of applications on top of almost infrastructural AI pieces.

66
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:42,070
The kind of testing that a foundational AI company has to do is.

67
00:04:42,070 --> 00:04:49,110
also different from what potentially an AI first company that uses AI infrastructure would do.

68
00:04:49,110 --> 00:04:56,070
and it would probably build upon the testing that a foundational AI company has in place. Is that a fair summarization?

69
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:59,189
Absolutely.

70
00:04:59,189 --> 00:05:01,510
I would say in building upon other people's models,

71
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:05,750
it's easy to sort of point the finger when something goes wrong and be able to say like, oh.

72
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:10,150
well, we're using this provider's model to do this part of our software stack.

73
00:05:10,150 --> 00:05:14,070
and therefore we can really only test inputs and outputs.

74
00:05:14,070 --> 00:05:18,390
Being on the foundational side, we really have the control to be able to go in.

75
00:05:18,390 --> 00:05:23,749
and tweak parameters or adjust the model itself in an attempt to design them out.

76
00:05:23,749 --> 00:05:26,150
rather than working around them.

77
00:05:26,150 --> 00:05:28,469
And that's a huge huge advantage.

78
00:05:28,469 --> 00:05:34,391
Yeah, I think a neat pattern that we've seen emerge is our customers are often times AI first. companies.

79
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:40,790
They're building upon deepgram as one of their foundational AI infrastructural pieces,

80
00:05:40,790 --> 00:05:43,670
but these are still AI companies offering AI enabled products.

81
00:05:43,670 --> 00:05:48,070
And so the kinds of testing that they do of our system is a certain kind of testing.

82
00:05:48,070 --> 00:05:49,990
in the AI world.

83
00:05:49,990 --> 00:05:51,830
One of the most common ways we see this.

84
00:05:51,830 --> 00:05:54,310
when we're talking with potential customers is is the bake off.

85
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:57,670
the classic bakeoff that's been in practice for decades across.

86
00:05:57,670 --> 00:06:04,309
so many industries where they take their actual production audio that they might want to turn into text.

87
00:06:04,309 --> 00:06:06,629
and run it through different providers.

88
00:06:06,629 --> 00:06:12,230
And despite what you may read about these different providers and whatever benchmarks they've previously published,

89
00:06:12,230 --> 00:06:13,749
really the thing.

90
00:06:13,749 --> 00:06:20,871
that matters most for these customers is how do the different options you're comparing do on your production. audio.

91
00:06:20,871 --> 00:06:23,110
Not on the benchmark set that you read a paper on,

92
00:06:23,110 --> 00:06:25,510
but how does it work for your customers?

93
00:06:25,510 --> 00:06:29,189
This is actually a place where we try to really thrive.

94
00:06:29,189 --> 00:06:31,909
because we play hard and compete hard in the customization space.

95
00:06:31,909 --> 00:06:35,830
and try to work with our customers in a really hightouch way in that manner.

96
00:06:35,830 --> 00:06:39,590
But that's a serious form of testing that has a lot of nuance.

97
00:06:39,590 --> 00:06:45,350
And then of course on the deepgram side we're thinking about that generalized across our entire customer base.

98
00:06:45,350 --> 00:06:47,270
All the different market segments that we're addressing,

99
00:06:47,270 --> 00:06:52,390
all the different domains that we seek to represent well in our modeling.

100
00:06:52,390 --> 00:06:55,990
So how exactly is testing ML models different from traditional software testing?

101
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,309
Well, I can say from the sort of data science side of the house,

102
00:07:00,309 --> 00:07:07,749
testing ML models for enterprise, enterprises tend to be far more riskaverse than your normal user.

103
00:07:07,749 --> 00:07:10,550
Enterprises have established software stacks.

104
00:07:10,550 --> 00:07:16,629
They generally have much stricter requirements around latency and accuracy,

105
00:07:16,629 --> 00:07:20,630
uptime, all of which need to be met in order for them to be able to serve their. products effectively.

106
00:07:20,630 --> 00:07:27,110
And enterprises typically span many use cases.

107
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:32,550
A single enterprise customer may contain and use our software for many different product lines.

108
00:07:32,550 --> 00:07:37,430
or for many different languages with very specific and niche applications.

109
00:07:37,430 --> 00:07:43,189
They may be serving internal customers or external customers.

110
00:07:43,189 --> 00:07:48,550
And this poses many operational and model development challenges that are really really interesting.

111
00:07:48,550 --> 00:07:54,070
One of the key differences here is that in traditional data science testing,

112
00:07:54,070 --> 00:07:59,189
as Jacob alluded to earlier, we generally have a quite welldefined and narrowly scoped problem.

113
00:07:59,189 --> 00:08:03,830
And to answer some sort of question, for example, maybe we're trying to make predictions about some data.

114
00:08:03,830 --> 00:08:08,230
How well can we classify the domain of some audio given some features about that audio?

115
00:08:08,230 --> 00:08:16,710
But when it comes to enterprises, often times there's a much higher level of system complexity both within.

116
00:08:16,710 --> 00:08:19,430
our system and the enterprise system.

117
00:08:19,430 --> 00:08:21,670
that requires testing of both on the model side,

118
00:08:21,670 --> 00:08:28,390
which is the side that I'm more familiar with, and on the production side of serving that.

119
00:08:28,390 --> 00:08:32,230
And so when a customer encounters a problem, there's a decent amount of testing.

120
00:08:32,230 --> 00:08:36,631
that first needs to happen on our side in order to determine did it happen somewhere on the. request side?

121
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:40,790
Was it an issue with how the user submitted audio?

122
00:08:40,790 --> 00:08:42,550
Is it something specific about the audio itself?

123
00:08:42,550 --> 00:08:46,150
Did something go wrong in the production stack?

124
00:08:46,150 --> 00:08:48,230
Or is it actually a fundamental issue with the model?

125
00:08:48,230 --> 00:08:52,550
There's a lot of interdependencies between the model and the production code.

126
00:08:52,550 --> 00:08:58,710
There are also interdependencies between Deepgram Grams models and how they're used by the enterprise customer.

127
00:08:58,710 --> 00:09:02,630
We've also got a much much larger scale of data that we're talking about here.

128
00:09:02,630 --> 00:09:09,990
Traditional data science, we may be talking about tens or hundreds of thousands, millions of test examples,

129
00:09:09,990 --> 00:09:15,030
but our stack is serving tens of thousands of requests per second.

130
00:09:15,030 --> 00:09:17,350
And so this poses very unique data capturing,

131
00:09:17,350 --> 00:09:21,430
storage, filtering challenges in actually targeting the right type of data.

132
00:09:21,430 --> 00:09:28,870
And then we've also got the case that the edge cases in which they're used are innumerable.

133
00:09:28,870 --> 00:09:34,949
There are many many parameters to investigate across user API parameters.

134
00:09:34,949 --> 00:09:42,390
that are specified to audio specific parameters that could be things about audio quality or acoustic conditions. that are present.

135
00:09:42,390 --> 00:09:46,070
Could be down to inference parameters that we have set up on the model itself.

136
00:09:46,070 --> 00:09:50,389
This could be things like the duration of the audio.

137
00:09:50,389 --> 00:09:52,630
Are people submitting very very short segments of audio.

138
00:09:52,630 --> 00:09:57,030
and expecting the same types of behavior as submitting minutelong clips?

139
00:09:57,030 --> 00:09:58,870
These are almost impossible to design out.

140
00:09:58,870 --> 00:10:01,590
And I'll let Jacob speak to some interesting cases.

141
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:04,470
that he's definitely run across on the edge case side.

142
00:10:04,480 --> 00:10:07,110
Yeah, we should get into some more stories here at some point, Connor.

143
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:08,710
That'll make for some fun listening.

144
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:16,710
Maybe briefly, Kchan, you asked a bit about how does this compare to software testing traditionally speaking.

145
00:10:16,710 --> 00:10:19,990
And one fun thing here is I have to say I only have a certain limited perspective.

146
00:10:19,990 --> 00:10:23,509
I haven't worked across the entire AI industry.

147
00:10:23,509 --> 00:10:26,389
I do have a useful comparison here.

148
00:10:26,389 --> 00:10:32,389
because before Deepgram I worked in kind of traditional software consulting doing typical big enterprise application development things.

149
00:10:32,389 --> 00:10:34,710
like payments processing.

150
00:10:34,710 --> 00:10:36,710
and record storage and that sort of thing.

151
00:10:36,710 --> 00:10:38,870
I think that one of the big ideas.

152
00:10:38,870 --> 00:10:43,751
that has emerged from software development very generally over the last few decades is we have a testing. pyramid.

153
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:46,710
And of course many of your listeners would be familiar with this.

154
00:10:46,710 --> 00:10:52,069
but you can walk through think about some application where there's a user interface.

155
00:10:52,069 --> 00:10:54,069
and the user can update his birthday.

156
00:10:54,069 --> 00:10:56,630
And there's probably going to be some unit testing.

157
00:10:56,630 --> 00:10:58,790
that says hey if I pass in this object.

158
00:10:58,790 --> 00:11:02,710
that represents a person's information it gets updated with the birthday.

159
00:11:02,710 --> 00:11:06,230
okay we can ensure that that part is correct. And maybe you have an integ integration test.

160
00:11:06,230 --> 00:11:12,949
that asserts that this object as you've modeled it in your code is correctly mapped into the domain.

161
00:11:12,949 --> 00:11:15,350
of your database schema.

162
00:11:15,350 --> 00:11:18,069
Maybe you have something like a higher level integration test.

163
00:11:18,069 --> 00:11:22,550
that asserts that all this works through a backend API.

164
00:11:22,550 --> 00:11:24,710
And then finally, like at the tippy top of your testing pyramid,

165
00:11:24,720 --> 00:11:29,990
the thing that's the slowest, most expensive to run, but really the most valuable for confirming.

166
00:11:29,990 --> 00:11:36,150
that things work is you have some sort of endto-end test, maybe running through Selenium in a browser,

167
00:11:36,150 --> 00:11:38,310
and you're testing that this works through the user UI.

168
00:11:38,310 --> 00:11:39,910
So that's your traditional testing pyramid.

169
00:11:39,910 --> 00:11:43,750
And really, in a sense, this testing pyramid doesn't go away.

170
00:11:43,750 --> 00:11:47,509
We still need to have lots of small test cases that are the foundation,

171
00:11:47,509 --> 00:11:51,990
and we build up to some more expensive ones that operate near the top.

172
00:11:51,990 --> 00:11:53,750
It's just that what those layers look like have really,

173
00:11:53,750 --> 00:12:01,430
really, really changed because we're no longer testing the storage and retrieval of data in a data model.

174
00:12:01,430 --> 00:12:04,790
which is the vast majority of probably software applications that have ever been written.

175
00:12:04,790 --> 00:12:07,350
And now we have this compute intensive world.

176
00:12:07,350 --> 00:12:09,030
So at our lowest level, at this unit level,

177
00:12:09,030 --> 00:12:15,190
now we're thinking about operations in a neural network and their mathematical correctness.

178
00:12:15,190 --> 00:12:17,190
In one sense, this is still a foundational unit test.

179
00:12:17,190 --> 00:12:19,269
We can model this in functional code.

180
00:12:19,269 --> 00:12:24,389
In another sense, it's kind of tricky because now you're taking on this more intense mathematical domain.

181
00:12:24,389 --> 00:12:26,310
But then you move up a level in the stack.

182
00:12:26,310 --> 00:12:31,190
and many of these lower level functions become a full models network.

183
00:12:31,190 --> 00:12:33,509
or maybe there's even a pipeline of models.

184
00:12:33,509 --> 00:12:35,509
whose inputs and outputs are wired up together.

185
00:12:35,509 --> 00:12:40,310
So now you want to test that that flow works.

186
00:12:40,310 --> 00:12:41,829
Probably in most AI systems you have a variety of features.

187
00:12:41,829 --> 00:12:47,830
And so now you're testing that that flow works with intersection of various parameters that users tune. Right?

188
00:12:47,830 --> 00:12:50,790
You see a lot of modern AI APIs.

189
00:12:50,790 --> 00:12:54,790
where a lot of power is put in the hands of the user to configure things under the.

190
00:12:54,790 --> 00:12:59,030
hood like the temperature used in different inference worlds.

191
00:12:59,030 --> 00:13:00,790
And so we're gradually working higher and higher up the stack.

192
00:13:00,790 --> 00:13:02,389
and you eventually get into the world of okay,

193
00:13:02,399 --> 00:13:07,590
we can confirm that we get exactly the right outputs.

194
00:13:07,590 --> 00:13:09,190
for exactly this one given input.

195
00:13:09,190 --> 00:13:13,670
And then you get into the data diversity explosion that Connor mentioned.

196
00:13:13,670 --> 00:13:16,470
the fact that in Deepgram's world, we're taking in arbitrary audio.

197
00:13:16,480 --> 00:13:20,790
and audio can be encoded in a lot of different ways.

198
00:13:20,790 --> 00:13:22,470
You can say all sorts of things within it.

199
00:13:22,470 --> 00:13:24,949
And so now you're trying to deal with that space.

200
00:13:24,949 --> 00:13:29,269
And then finally, you get into I think this is the most powerful world for Deepgram.

201
00:13:29,269 --> 00:13:31,190
and our customers to collaborate on.

202
00:13:31,190 --> 00:13:38,710
but it's the downstream integrations between say a foundational AI system and our customers systems.

203
00:13:38,710 --> 00:13:44,230
We've even seen cases where you can have AI outputs that improve.

204
00:13:44,230 --> 00:13:47,350
But if say Deepgram's speechtoext outputs are.

205
00:13:47,350 --> 00:13:50,550
then used by a customer for some sort of NLP process.

206
00:13:50,560 --> 00:13:55,030
and so now they're doing something like looking for product names mentioned.

207
00:13:55,030 --> 00:13:59,910
or key topics discussed in internal meetings.

208
00:13:59,910 --> 00:14:03,670
Well, if your transcription output changes significantly in some way,

209
00:14:03,670 --> 00:14:07,269
then you might have made an improvement but thrown off a downstream model.

210
00:14:07,269 --> 00:14:10,069
And so you actually care about that very full endto-end flow.

211
00:14:10,069 --> 00:14:13,590
and that it's the same testing pyramid but it's just cast in a new light.

212
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:18,389
Thanks for highlighting all those challenges.

213
00:14:18,389 --> 00:14:21,189
So who exactly is responsible for doing this testing?

214
00:14:21,189 --> 00:14:23,750
Is it the data scientist?

215
00:14:23,750 --> 00:14:26,310
Is it the ML engineer?

216
00:14:26,310 --> 00:14:29,511
I've heard this new term of an AI engineer. So who is it? Which role?

217
00:14:29,519 --> 00:14:34,629
I wish there was a single answer for you.

218
00:14:34,629 --> 00:14:38,470
Unfortunately or fortunately, the first part of this process is always to identify.

219
00:14:38,470 --> 00:14:41,590
where exactly something is going wrong.

220
00:14:41,590 --> 00:14:45,750
And that comes with high levels of observability throughout the system,

221
00:14:45,750 --> 00:14:51,351
whether that's in the production system or whether that's within the model. Who owns it?

222
00:14:51,351 --> 00:14:55,590
Well, when we've identified where in the system things are going wrong,

223
00:14:55,590 --> 00:14:59,269
then that sort of dictates whether it's more on the ML engineer, let's say.

224
00:14:59,269 --> 00:15:04,069
or on the engineering side or on the research side.

225
00:15:04,069 --> 00:15:10,310
If it's a fundamental issue with the model where the model is producing hallucinations under some bad conditions,

226
00:15:10,310 --> 00:15:12,790
then that warrants a retrain and.

227
00:15:12,790 --> 00:15:17,990
that warrants basically a revision to the model to ensure that that doesn't happen.

